{-
   The tail-read guarantee, as a program that would fault if it did not hold - Design-Vector §5.4 and
   Implementation-Vector.md §8.

   A vector loop's last iteration reads a whole register where only part of it is wanted. What makes
   that safe is not that the bytes past the end mean anything - they are unspecified, and nothing
   here looks at what they hold - but that they are *mapped*: storage the language allocated has a
   vector's width of readable memory after it. This reads `kMaxVectorBytes - 1` bytes past the end of
   every size class the allocator has, and past a block that ends exactly at a region's limit, and
   the whole of what it asserts is that the process is still running afterwards.

   It has to be a runnable fixture for that reason. A golden file cannot say "this load did not
   fault", and the guarantee has no other symptom: on a heap whose regions are mapped to their stated
   size the read succeeds for every block but the last few in a region, and for those it takes down
   the process. Which is why the second half below is the important one - the first would pass
   against an unguarded heap almost always.

   `Native` rather than a container, because what carries the guarantee is the *allocator* and the
   point is to test it directly: an `Array(a)` would reach the same blocks with an extra layer of
   ownership between the test and the thing being tested.
-}
import Native

-- One byte past the end, read as a byte at a time so that nothing here depends on a vector type
-- existing yet. 63 is `kMaxVectorBytes - 1`, which is the widest overread the language permits.
let guardWidth = 63 :: I64

-- Reads the guard bytes after `size` bytes of `block` and folds them together, so that no optimizer
-- can decide the loads are dead. The value is meaningless by construction - the bytes are
-- unspecified - and only the fact that they were readable is being asserted.
fn readPast(block: %U8, size: I64) -> I64:
    let &sum = 0 :: I64
    let &i = 0 :: I64

    while i < guardWidth:
        sum = sum + (*(block + (size + i)) :: I64)
        i = i + 1

    return sum

fn main() -> Int:
    {-
       Every size class the allocator has, from 16 bytes up. Each block is read past its own end,
       which inside a region is free - the next block's header follows it - and is what this half
       establishes rather than tests.
    -}
    let &request = 8 :: I64
    let &seen = 0 :: I64

    while request < 1048576:
        let block = allocateHeap(request)
        if isNull(block) then return -1

        -- Discarded, but read: the point is the load, and a sum nothing looks at is one the
        -- optimizer is entitled to remove.
        if readPast(block, request) < 0 then return -2

        seen = seen + 1
        request = request + request

    if seen != 17 then return -3

    {-
       The case the guard page is for: a block that ends at the region's limit.

       Allocated until the bump pointer is within a vector's width of the limit, then read past the
       last block. Without the guard the read is off the end of the mapping and the process dies with
       a fault rather than answering anything; with it, the bytes are there and unspecified.

       The loop takes 16-byte blocks so that it lands close to the limit rather than jumping over it
       into a new region, and it is bounded because `growHeap` moves the limit: reaching it once is
       all this needs, and a region is 4 MiB of 16-byte blocks.
    -}
    let &last = null() :: %U8
    let &steps = 0 :: I64

    while difference(heapNext, heapLimit) > guardWidth:
        let block = allocateHeap(8)
        if isNull(block) then return -4

        last = block
        steps = steps + 1

        -- The bump pointer only fails to move when a free list handed the block back, which cannot
        -- happen here because nothing is freed. A bound on the loop rather than a proof of it.
        if steps > 300000 then return -5

    if isNull(last) then return -6
    if readPast(last, 8) < 0 then return -7

    return 0
